  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework was evaluated exclusively on a synthetic grid topology with a small fleet size of 100 vehicles, leaving its performance on larger fleets and actual road network geometries unverified.",
      "what": "This study introduces a two-stage dynamic charging framework for electric ride-hailing fleets, combining a day-ahead planner that minimizes expected energy costs and queuing delays with an online reservation-based request scheduler that dynamically dispatches vehicles and assigns chargers. Tested on a synthetic Manhattan-like grid using NYC yellow taxi demand patterns across 100-vehicle fleets and daily loads of 3,000 to 4,000 customers, the model achieved up to a 21.68% increase in fleet profits and a 22.22% higher service rate compared to basic benchmark strategies under high-demand scenarios.",
      "why": "It replaces static or uncoordinated charging routines with a real-time reservation scheme that dynamically balances grid congestion against fleet revenue opportunities under uncertain passenger demand."
    }
  },
